  "universes": 1,
  "notes": "<div><font size=\"5\"><b>Looks and cues</b></font></div><div>A small theatre stage with eight lamps. Six looks are stored in memories, and the stack plays them one after the other as the cues of a play.</div><div><b><font color=\"#ffb020\">What is in it</font></b></div><ul><li>Channels 1 to 8: one lamp each. The note under a channel fader says which; <b>Stage</b> shows where it hangs, in the color of its gel.</li><li>Memories 1 to 6: house light, warm wash, blue night, a spot, a sunset and the full stage. Each holds a look: the levels of the channels.</li><li>The stack: nine cues. Each brings a memory up over its up time; the next cue takes it down over its down time.</li></ul><div><b><font color=\"#ffb020\">Try it</font></b></div><ol><li>On <b>Main</b>, push a channel fader up, then a memory fader: the memory gives its look, and the higher level of the two wins.</li><li>Open <b>Memories</b> to see which levels each memory holds.</li><li>Press <b>Stack</b>: it lights up, the stack is live and stands on its first cue. <b>GO</b> (or the space bar) goes to the next cue, <b>Back</b> to the one before. Cue 6.0 goes on by itself after its wait.</li><li>Watch <b>Stage</b> while the stack plays: the lamps show the light they give.</li><li>Make a look of your own: set the channel faders, press <b>Save mem</b>, then a free memory key. <b>Add cue</b> on the <b>Stack</b> view puts it into the stack.</li><li><b>Cue sheet</b> on the <b>Stack</b> view prints the cues, for whoever calls them.</li></ol><div>Press <b>Stack</b> again to stop the show: while it is live, nothing that is stored can be changed.</div>",
